{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued a formal correction regarding the geographical scope of recent legislation concerning pet theft. While an initial report stated that the new law making the abduction of cats and dogs a specific criminal offense applies across the entire United Kingdom, this was inaccurate. The updated clarification specifies that these legal changes are currently in force only within England and Northern Ireland, leaving out Scotland and Wales from this specific statutory provision.\n\n***\n\nSection 2\nFurther amendments address several factual errors found in previous editions, ranging from corporate titles to religious identities and personal names. One key correction involves Eileen Burbidge, who was mistakenly identified as the founder of Fertifa when she actually serves as the executive director, with the true founders being Tony Chen and Nick Kuan. Additionally, the publication corrected the religious background of the family featured in the film Bend It Like Beckham from Muslim to Sikh, and rectified the name of director Alex Lawther, which had been incorrectly printed as Lowther.\n\nSection 3\n* The newspaper also clarified a historical detail regarding Venetia Stanley's family, noting that it was her uncle, the third Baron Stanley of Alderley, who converted to Islam, rather than her father as previously reported in the article about her relationship with Prime Minister Herbert Asquith.\n* A list of other recently amended articles was published, covering diverse topics such as the arrest of Telegram founder Pavel Durov, new exhibitions on royal servants, Pope Francis's reaction to England's cricket strategy, potential British sci-tech dominance, the missing Salvator Mundi painting, and a review of summer wines.\n\nSection 4\nReaders wishing to subm